Q: Avoid layout shift when changing font weight A: <p>To ensure that this works for any word length, you can have the same text twice (also without duplicate code, see below): once always in bold to reserve the space but hidden and <a href="https://css-tricks.com/how-to-stack-elements-in-css/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">stacked</a>, and once reactive to hover:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>div {
  display: flex;
}

nav {
  display: grid;
  gap: 1rem;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
  font: 120% system-ui;
  text-align: center;
  padding: 2rem 1rem;
}
nav a {
  text-decoration: none;
  color: black;
  grid-row: 1;
  transition: 200ms;
}
a:hover, .placeholder {
  font-weight: 900;
}
nav :nth-child(4n+1) { grid-column: 1; }
nav :nth-child(4n+2) { grid-column: 2; }
nav :nth-child(4n+3) { grid-column: 3; }
nav :nth-child(4n+4) { grid-column: 4; }
.placeholder {
  visibility: hidden;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;nav&gt;
    &lt;a href=""&gt;One&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a href=""&gt;Two&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a href=""&gt;Three&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a href=""&gt;Four&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a class="placeholder" href=""&gt;One&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a class="placeholder" href=""&gt;Two&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a class="placeholder" href=""&gt;Three&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a class="placeholder" href=""&gt;Four&lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;/nav&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>The placeholders should have the same text and location as the visible text. One way to facilitate that is to synchronize their text using <code>content</code> (but then browser page translate might not translate that text):</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="true" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code snippet-currently-hidden">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>div {
  display: flex;
}

nav {
  display: grid;
  gap: 1rem;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr));
  font: 120% system-ui;
  text-align: center;
  padding: 2rem 1rem;
}
nav a {
  text-decoration: none;
  color: black;
  grid-row: 1;
  transition: 200ms;
}
a:hover, .placeholder {
  font-weight: 900;
}
nav :nth-child(4n+1) { grid-column: 1; }
nav :nth-child(4n+2) { grid-column: 2; }
nav :nth-child(4n+3) { grid-column: 3; }
nav :nth-child(4n+4) { grid-column: 4; }
nav :nth-child(4n+1):after { content: 'One'; }
nav :nth-child(4n+2):after { content: 'Two'; }
nav :nth-child(4n+3):after { content: 'Three'; }
nav :nth-child(4n+4):after { content: 'Four'; }
.placeholder {
  visibility: hidden;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div&gt;
  &lt;nav&gt;
    &lt;a href=""&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a href=""&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a href=""&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a href=""&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a class="placeholder" href=""&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a class="placeholder" href=""&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a class="placeholder" href=""&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a class="placeholder" href=""&gt;&lt;/a&gt;
  &lt;/nav&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Regarding the issue that variables cannot be obtained using @forward in CSS preprocessor Sass A: <p>You can import as follows:</p>
<pre><code>@use '@/assets/style/variables.scss' as * with ($theme: light);
</code></pre>
<p><code>@forward</code> isn't needed unless you want other files to import variables through <code>sidebar.scss</code></p>
<p>Edit:</p>
<p>Expose variables in your Vue SFC:</p>
<pre><code>&lt;style lang=&quot;scss&quot; scoped&gt;
@use '@/assets/style/variables.scss' with ($theme: light);
@use '@/components/SidebarMenu/SidebarMenu.scss';
&lt;/style&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>In your <code>sidebar.scss</code>:</p>
<pre><code>@use '@/assets/style/variables.scss' as *;
</code></pre>

Q: SELECT...INTO in execute block is returning the wrong value A: <blockquote>
<p>When I added identifier around the :fieldgroup variable, it gave me the right answer. I had though identifier was only needed for table objects. I'm a little confused now as to when identifier needs to be added around a variable</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It is an example of how variable is interpreted as a value or as an identifier. Using a simpler case:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>DECLARE
    row_count string;
    fieldgroup varchar default 'claim_num';
BEGIN
  select :fieldgroup  
  into :row_count 
  from (select 1) AS s(claim_num);

  return :row_count;
END;

-- claim_num
</code></pre>
<p>vs.</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>DECLARE
    row_count string;
    fieldgroup varchar default 'claim_num';
BEGIN
  select identifier(:fieldgroup)
  into :row_count 
  from (select 1) AS s(claim_num);

  return :row_count;
END;

-- 1
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Returning to the original queries, skipping dynamic part they are as follows:</p>
<pre><code>select count(1)
from raw.claims.s_clm_carrier_claim
group by 'claim_num'  -- string (which is effectively a single group)
having count(1) &gt; 1;

select count(1)
from raw.claims.s_clm_carrier_claim
group by claim_num   -- identifier - column name
having count(1) &gt; 1;
</code></pre>
<p><strong><a href="https://dbfiddle.uk/lgtcl_gH" rel="nofollow noreferrer">db&lt;&gt;fiddle demo</a></strong></p>
<p>Final query:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>DECLARE
    row_count integer;
    table_name varchar default 'raw.claims.s_clm_carrier_claim';
    fieldgroup varchar default 'claim_num';
BEGIN
  select count(1) 
  into :row_count 
  from identifier(:table_name) 
  group by IDENTIFIER(:fieldgroup )
  having count(1) &gt; 1;
  return :row_count;
END;
</code></pre>
<p>More at: <a href="https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/identifier-literal" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Literals and variables as identifiers with IDENTIFIER() syntax</a></p>

Q: Is it possible in HTML5 to give translation service a hint for translating a specific element A: <p>You can add <strong>invisible and not selectable/copyable</strong> text that provides context such that the translation is correct. For example, instead of &quot;train&quot;, you can write &quot;railway: train&quot; or &quot;sports training: train&quot; but make only the text &quot;train&quot; visible.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;style&gt;
.hint-for-translation {
  zoom: 0.01;
  font-size: 0.01px;
  user-select: none;
}
font {
  /* Hide the space character that Google Page Translate puts in
  the &lt;font&gt; elements that it adds. You can change this rule depending
  on what looks best with your page layout. */
  display: inline-block;
}
&lt;/style&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span class="hint-for-translation"&gt;railway: &lt;/span&gt;train&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;&lt;span class="hint-for-translation"&gt;sports training: &lt;/span&gt;train&lt;/div&gt;

Warning: Chrome currently doesn't translate outputs of Stack Overflow snippets.
You have to save the code to an HTML file.</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>To keep track of whether Google Translate keeps inserting <code>&lt;font&gt;</code>: <a href="https://issues.chromium.org/issues/41407169" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://issues.chromium.org/issues/41407169</a></p>
<p>Alternative variants:</p>
<ul>
<li><p>If you <strong>omit the colon &quot;:&quot;</strong>, the hint might get translated as an adjective of the main text, and in languages with a different word order, the translatin of the main text gets pulled into the front of the hint <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> (due to a bug in the translation software) and thus becomes invisible. So don't omit the colon.</p>
<ul>
<li>If you don't mind displaying the hints after translation and only want to hide them before translation, you can avoid said problem. Chrome translation adds <code>&lt;font&gt;</code> elements, so you can:
<pre><code>.hint-for-translation:has(font) {
  zoom: revert;
  font-size: revert;
}
</code></pre>
(Related: <code>inherit</code>, <code>initial</code>.)
But it's risky because it relies on them continuing to use <code>&lt;font&gt;</code> like that.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><p>Similarly, the translator translates <strong>hashtags</strong> not as hashtags but as adjectives into some languages, leading to the same problem.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Putting hints into <strong>brackets</strong> worked in my tests, but I expect similar risks for some other text.</p>
</li>
<li><p>So using <strong>a colon &quot;:&quot; seems best</strong> so far.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Brave Translate has a bug: it seems to translate each text node separately, ignoring context from elements like <code>&lt;span&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;mark&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;i&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;em&gt;</code>. Assigning which word got translated approximately to which word might require a big rework of their software. In the meantime, you can have a <code>.hint-for-translation</code> element saying &quot;railway:&quot;, another one saying &quot;railway: train&quot;, and a visible element saying &quot;train&quot;, use JS to watch for translations as in another answer here, and set the content of the visible element to the translation of &quot;railway: train&quot; <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/10398931/how-to-remove-text-from-a-string">minus</a> that of &quot;railway:&quot;. Or remove everything before the colon from the translation of &quot;railway: train&quot; if you can take into consideration how the colon gets translated into all relevant languages, for example <code>：</code>.</p>
